WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.960
My name is
Allan Blanchard I'm from Long

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.640
Island, New York, an undergrad
at MIT, and I'm studying

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.620
materials science and
engineering.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.660
UROP stands for Undergraduate
Research

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.600
Opportunities Program.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.600
They are really giving you the
resources to put any idea down

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.130
on the table and experiment
with it.

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:31.050
I open up literature and I read,
and I'm just like,

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.670
you know if I take A, B, and
C, and I put them together?

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:34.610
Do you think this could work?

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.610
They are just like I don't know,
let's just try it out

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:38.565
and see what's happening
and it's something new.

00:00:38.565 --> 00:00:40.960
It's something, that when you
think about it, is something

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.660
that's your own too.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.500
So, that's really what
makes it fun.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:50.430
I wanted to do something in
regards to the energy problem.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:53.120
With alternative energy sources,
like solar and wind,

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:54.550
they're intermittent.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:57.230
When there's lots of sun, and
you have a storage device to

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.300
store that energy, and give
it to you when there's

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.220
not a lot of sun, then
you can make this a

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.770
feasible energy solution.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.010
But the thing now is the storage
devices we have, like

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:09.070
lithium ion, they're incredibly
expensive.

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:12.430
So it's almost $1,000 per
kilowatt hour in some cases.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:15.670
You need a device that's $100
per kilowatt hour, so that's

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:20.650
10 times cheaper to make this
a feasible concept.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:22.070
So the liquid metal battery.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.160
The key concept behind this is
you're going to have three

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.660
layers, all in the
liquid state.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:29.770
You're going to have on the
bottom a dense metal.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:34.790
The layer in the middle,
that's the molten salt.

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:37.190
And then, the top layer
is the light metal.

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.370
The beauty behind the entire
system really is that you can

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:46.510
put them all in the same cell,
heat them up, and they're

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:48.800
going to assemble by their
relative densities of each of

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.960
these components.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.050
What I've been working on is
making the battery more

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:54.430
robust, more efficient.

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:57.450
I'm exploring just new
chemistries in general.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.820
When you have all the components
ready, you take the

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.530
cell, and this is where you
do a lot of your work.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:03.890
It's full with argon.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.970
And argon is an inert gas, so
you get really accurate

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:08.915
results, and also, you make sure
that you're safe because

00:02:08.915 --> 00:02:11.370
nothing really explodes
in this environment.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.660
However, there is some
drawbacks, in the sense that

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:16.310
When you're working with the
gloves here it takes about two

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:20.530
to three times longer
to do any process.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:25.610
We tried to use an entirely new
category of electrolytes.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:29.140
We were really lucky to get
some really great data.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:31.580
And now, we're trying to push
it even further to see if we

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:35.970
can increase the capacity
in a multitude of ways.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.330
We developed something that
is close to $100 per

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:39.390
kilowatt hour now.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:41.590
So it's bringing in the
alternative energy sources and

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:44.150
also it's going to allow the
grid to be a lot more energy

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:47.060
efficient, so it's cutting
on both regimes.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:50.180
And when you see that come
together, that's like a pretty

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:53.510
amazing feat.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:56.670
With this new device, it's going
to spark a new economy,

00:02:56.670 --> 00:03:00.280
because private homes could even
store energy at night and

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:03.810
sell it back to the grid
the next morning.

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:05.570
I think a lot of the consistency
that's been in my

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:08.090
undergraduate experience has
been in research and getting

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.045
the skills to learn
how to solve new

00:03:10.045 --> 00:03:11.295
and challenging problems.

